{
  "image_name": "4c4ca281969eebb8ebd65d1d013ff05b.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how PKM2 shifts from a cytoplasmic glycolytic enzyme to a nuclear regulator of gene expression, linking signaling, transcription, cell-cycle control, and the Warburg effect. In the cytoplasm, PKM2 is modified by several upstream factors: ERK1/2 and PIN1 promote phosphorylation-dependent isomerization from a tetramer to a monomer and expose a nuclear localization signal, while p300 acetylation and JMJD5 also favor nuclear entry. PKM2 then associates with importin-α5 and translocates into the nucleus.\n\nInside the nucleus, PKM2 appears to act as a co-regulator for multiple transcriptional programs. One route shows PHD3-dependent interaction with HIF1, together with p300, to activate HIF1 target genes. Another shows PKM2 cooperating with β-catenin/TCF-LEF and influencing histone H3T11 phosphorylation and HDAC3 removal, which promotes expression of genes such as MYC and CCND1 and supports the G1/S transition. PKM2 is also linked to STAT3 activation through phosphorylation at Y705.\n\nThe outputs of these nuclear events feed back to metabolism: genes promoting glucose uptake and glycolysis, including GLUT1 and LDHA, are induced, reinforcing conversion of glucose through PEP and pyruvate to lactate. Overall, the image depicts PKM2 as both a metabolic enzyme and a nuclear signaling hub in cancer-like metabolic reprogramming.",
